# Commit and push after updates

When you finish work that changes this repository (code, config, Prisma schema/migrations, docs, or `.cursor/rules`), publish it to GitHub before ending the turn.

**Remote:** `origin` → https://github.com/AIChief-Courtney/cardscanner.git, branch `main`.

## When to commit

- Commit after a coherent chunk of work (a feature, fix, or docs update)—not after every single line edit mid-task.
- If the user only asked a question with no file changes, do not commit.

## What never to commit

- `.env`, secrets, credentials
- `node_modules/`, `.next/`, `*.log`, build caches (e.g. `*.tsbuildinfo`)

## Commit quality

1. Run `git status` and `git diff` (staged and unstaged) before committing.
2. Split unrelated changes into separate commits when practical.
3. Use conventional prefixes: `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `docs`, `refactor`.
4. Subject line: imperative, ~72 chars. Body: what changed and why (1–3 sentences).
5. Do not amend pushed commits; do not force-push `main` unless the user explicitly asks.

## Push

1. After one or more new commits, run `git push origin main` (or `git push` when tracking is set).
2. If push fails (auth, non-fast-forward), report the error and what the user should do; retry after a pull/rebase only if safe and requested.
3. Briefly tell the user the commit subject(s) and that `main` is up to date on GitHub.

This rule applies even when general guidance says “commit only when asked”—for this repo, publishing updates is expected unless the user says otherwise in the same message.